      "latin": "corruō, corruere, corruī",
      "status": "recommended",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "fall or collapse",
      "comment": "Useful when “crash” means coming down in a heap rather than hitting another object. Traupman also applies it to a computer crash; that is a separate modern extension.",
      "evidence": [
        "Traupman 260, s.v. crash (intr.)",
        "Traupman 298"
      ]
    },
    {
      "latin": "ruō, ruere, ruī, rutum",
      "status": "alternative",
      "period": "classical",
      "gloss": "fall with a crash, tumble down, go to ruin; also rush headlong",
      "evidence": [
        "Liv. 21.11 (Lewis &amp; Short)",
        "Verg. A. 6.305: <span lang=\"la\">hūc omnis turba ruēbat</span> (Lewis &amp; Short)"
      ],
      "comment": "Of walls, towers and houses that come down; then of hasty movement, to rush (Lewis &amp; Short)."
    }
  ],
